  <div class="space-y-8 mt-2">
    <section class="rounded-[12px] border border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated p-4 sm:p-8 shadow-whisper space-y-4">
      <span class="mono-eyebrow text-link">Guide</span>
      <h2 class="heading-md text-ink">What Is an Online Ruler?</h2>
      <div class="space-y-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          An online ruler is a measuring scale rendered on your screen that represents real physical distance — centimeters, millimeters, or inches. Place a flat object against the edge of the scale and read its size the same way you would with a physical ruler. Because every display packs pixels differently, a good online ruler is also a calibration tool: it needs to know how many pixels correspond to one inch on <em>your</em> screen before its numbers mean anything.
        </p>
        <p>
          This page combines the ruler, the calibration system, and a direct measurement mode in one place. Everything runs locally in your browser — no downloads, no uploads, no account.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="rounded-[12px] border border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated p-4 sm:p-8 shadow-whisper space-y-4">
      <span class="mono-eyebrow text-link">How to</span>
      <h2 class="heading-md text-ink">How to Use the Online Ruler</h2>
      <ol class="space-y-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed list-decimal list-inside">
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Calibrate first.</strong> Click <em>Calibrate</em> and match the on-screen outline to a bank card held against your display. This takes under a minute and makes every measurement meaningful.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Pick your unit.</strong> Switch between cm, mm, inches, or raw pixels with the unit control (keys 1–4).</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Measure edge-to-edge.</strong> Place the object against the top or left ruler and read the value at its far edge. Enable extra edges from the edge controls.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Or measure anywhere.</strong> Turn on <em>Measure</em>, then click and drag across the stage to measure any distance — the result shows your unit plus conversions.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Use guides for comparisons.</strong> Enable <em>Guides</em> and drag the dashed lines to mark positions; each guide shows its live position in the active unit.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="text-xs text-mute font-mono">Keep browser zoom at 100% while measuring — zoom changes the scale. The tool shows a warning when it detects a zoomed viewport.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="rounded-[12px] border border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated p-4 sm:p-8 shadow-whisper space-y-4">
      <span class="mono-eyebrow text-link">Calibration</span>
      <h2 class="heading-md text-ink">How to Calibrate the Ruler</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-xs sm:text-sm">
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 space-y-1.5">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink font-mono">1 · Physical card or object</h3>
          <p class="text-body leading-relaxed">Hold a standard bank card (85.60 × 53.98 mm), a sheet of A4 or US Letter paper, or a coin against the outline and resize the outline until the edges match. This is a direct screen-to-object reference and the most trustworthy method.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 space-y-1.5">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink font-mono">2 · Screen diagonal</h3>
          <p class="text-body leading-relaxed">Enter your display's diagonal (e.g. 13.3″, 15.6″, 27″) and resolution. The engine computes pixels-per-inch from the Pythagorean diagonal of your pixel grid — solid when you know the panel size.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 space-y-1.5">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink font-mono">3 · Device preset</h3>
          <p class="text-body leading-relaxed">Pick a device category — iPhone, iPad, Android, MacBook, Windows laptop, or desktop monitor. Presets use typical panel geometry for each category; they beat a blind guess but are not model-exact.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas p-4 space-y-1.5">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink font-mono">4 · Auto estimate</h3>
          <p class="text-body leading-relaxed">The fastest option. The tool reads your reported screen resolution and pixel ratio and assumes a typical diagonal for your device class. It is clearly labeled as an estimate — because it is one.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-mute font-mono">Your calibration is saved in this browser only (namespace fac-online-ruler:v1) and restores automatically on your next visit.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="rounded-[12px] border border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated p-4 sm:p-8 shadow-whisper space-y-4">
      <span class="mono-eyebrow text-link">Units</span>
      <h2 class="heading-md text-ink">Online Ruler in Centimeters, Millimeters and Inches</h2>
      <div class="space-y-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          The cm ruler divides each centimeter into ten millimeter ticks with bold centimeter marks — the everyday choice for crafts, school, and sewing. The mm ruler labels every ten millimeters and is the finest scale, ideal for coins, screws, and jewelry. The inch ruler shows whole inches with eighth and sixteenth subdivisions, matching how imperial tape measures and rulers are read. A fourth mode, px, measures in screen pixels and needs no calibration at all — it is exact by definition and useful for checking on-screen layouts and print-size mockups.
        </p>
        <p>
          All four scales come from one conversion engine built on the exact definitions: 1 inch = 25.4 mm and 1 cm = 10 mm. Every readout also shows the equivalent values in the other units, so a single measurement gives you the full conversion at once.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="rounded-[12px] border border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated p-4 sm:p-8 shadow-whisper space-y-4">
      <span class="mono-eyebrow text-link">Devices</span>
      <h2 class="heading-md text-ink">Can You Use Your Phone or Laptop as a Ruler?</h2>
      <div class="space-y-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Yes — both work well within the limits of their screen size. On a phone, the ruler spans roughly 6–9 cm or 2.5–3.5 inches, which covers coins, stamps, screws, jewelry, and most craft work. Rotate to landscape for the longer span, drag guides with your finger, and use card calibration sized for phone screens. On a laptop or desktop monitor you get a much longer measuring edge (typically 25–60 cm), which handles envelopes, books, cables, and paper sizes end-to-end.
        </p>
        <p>
          The practical limit is the same for every screen: you can only measure as long as the visible ruler. For longer objects, mark the end with a guide, slide the object, and add the two readings.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="rounded-[12px] border border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated p-4 sm:p-8 shadow-whisper space-y-4">
      <span class="mono-eyebrow text-link">Honesty</span>
      <h2 class="heading-md text-ink">How Accurate Is an Online Ruler?</h2>
      <div class="space-y-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          An uncalibrated online ruler is an estimate. Browsers cannot detect your screen's physical dimensions, so the starting scale assumes a typical pixels-per-inch value that may be off by several percent on your device. Calibration fixes this: matching a bank card gives the scale a direct physical reference, and entering your screen's true diagonal grounds the math in real geometry.
        </p>
        <p>
          What still affects results: <strong class="text-ink">browser zoom</strong> (reset to 100%), <strong class="text-ink">operating-system display scaling</strong> (which changes how the browser reports pixels), <strong class="text-ink">alignment precision</strong> (a card edge that is half a millimeter off shifts the whole scale), and <strong class="text-ink">panel manufacturing variance</strong> (nominal sizes are rounded). We do not publish an accuracy percentage because none would be scientifically honest — instead, the status indicator above the ruler always tells you exactly which confidence level the current scale has: estimated, calibrated with device data, or calibrated with a physical reference.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

